# =============================================================================
# RepOS — environment template
# =============================================================================
# Copy this file to `.env.local` and fill in real values.
# `.env.local` is gitignored and MUST never be committed.
#
# Every value here is a PLACEHOLDER. Nothing in this file is a real credential,
# and nothing real may ever be committed into it.
#
# Nothing in this file is exposed to the browser. RepOS deliberately defines
# ZERO `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variables — any variable prefixed with NEXT_PUBLIC_ would
# be inlined into client bundles, so we never put a key behind that prefix.
# That includes Supabase: authentication runs entirely in server actions, so
# the project URL and anon key stay server-side like everything else.
# =============================================================================

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Database — Supabase PostgreSQL (M20)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TWO urls, not one, and they are not interchangeable.
#
#   DATABASE_URL         the Transaction Pooler, port 6543. What the running
#                        application uses. `pgbouncer=true` is REQUIRED:
#                        transaction-mode pooling cannot hold the prepared
#                        statements Prisma would otherwise create, and without
#                        it queries fail intermittently under load rather than
#                        immediately — the worst way to find out.
#                        `connection_limit` is a POOL SIZE, and 1 is the wrong
#                        answer for a long-running server. Every page loads
#                        several independent things with Promise.all; with one
#                        connection they queue instead, and the client detail
#                        page measured 2,520 ms serialised against 551 ms with
#                        room to run - the same queries either way. Use 1 only
#                        on a serverless platform, where each invocation is its
#                        own process and a larger pool per instance would
#                        exhaust the pooler. It also keeps a serverless instance from
#                        opening a pool per invocation.
#
#   DIRECT_DATABASE_URL  the Session Pooler, port 5432. Used ONLY by Prisma
#                        migrations and by scripts/bootstrap-admin.mjs. Schema
#                        work needs a real session; the transaction pooler
#                        cannot give it one.
#
#   PUBLIC_DATABASE_URL  the customer feedback page, and nothing else.
#
# There is deliberately no default for any of them. A default is how a
# migration command ends up aimed at whatever happened to be at a guessed path.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres?pgbouncer=true&connection_limit=1"
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres"

# The anonymous gateway connection (M20 Stage 10B).
#
# A customer scanning a QR code has no account, so Row Level Security — which
# decides what a request may see from the signed-in person's identity — would
# correctly show them nothing, including the one page they are entitled to use.
# Rather than weaken a policy for them, that page connects as `repos_public`:
# a role with NO privilege on any table, which may call exactly two functions.
# Each takes the customer's token, resolves the business from the token itself,
# and returns one projection. There is no argument through which a caller can
# name a business, so a stolen token reaches one gateway or none.
#
# What this connection string is worth if it leaks: the ability to post
# feedback to a gateway whose token the holder already had. Not one row of
# anyone's data. It is still a secret, and it is still server-side only —
# never NEXT_PUBLIC_, never imported by a client component.
#
# REQUIRED IN PRODUCTION. With NODE_ENV=production and this empty, RepOS
# refuses to build the anonymous handle at all and the feedback page returns an
# error, naming this variable in the server log. That is deliberate: it used to
# fall back to the ordinary application client instead, which left every
# operator screen healthy while every printed QR code pointed at a page whose
# security boundary was gone — a failure with no symptom is worse than an
# outage. Get the value by running prisma/m20/public-gateway.sql and then
# setting the repos_public password with \password (see prisma/m20/README.md).
#
# Leave it empty in development and in the test suite, where one role owns
# everything; the gateway then uses the ordinary client and says so on stderr.
PUBLIC_DATABASE_URL=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase Auth (M20) — the ONLY identity system
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RepOS stores no password, no password hash, no session secret and no reset
# token. Supabase Auth verifies every sign-in; RepOS holds a User row, the id
# of the Supabase identity behind it (User.authProviderId), and the memberships
# that decide what that person may reach.
#
# Both values come from Project Settings -> API. They are read only on the
# server, by src/lib/auth/supabase.ts.
SUPABASE_URL="https://<project-ref>.supabase.co"
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=""

# The service-role key bypasses Row Level Security completely. RepOS does not
# read it anywhere, and it must not be added here or to any deployment
# environment unless a future server-only job genuinely needs it.
# SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# First administrator (M20)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Platform admin cannot be granted from a browser, from a signup or from an
# invitation — the database refuses that column to the application role.
# scripts/bootstrap-admin.mjs is the only path, and it demands this secret in
# the environment AND as --secret. At least 16 characters.
#
# Set it, run the script once against an account that has already signed up
# through /signup, then rotate or remove it.
REPOS_BOOTSTRAP_SECRET=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public feedback address (M14/M19)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The origin every customer-facing QR code and printed card resolves to. Leave
# blank to fall back to the operator setting, then to the request origin.
# In production this must be https.
REPOS_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AI provider (OPTIONAL)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# RepOS works fully without any AI key. With none configured it runs in
# deterministic-only mode: feedback is tagged by the local keyword classifier
# and report prose falls back to templates. AI never computes a number.
#
# V1 registers exactly ONE provider: Groq. Google-operated endpoints are
# prohibited by the V1 hard rules, so there is no Gemini provider in this
# build. An unknown value here is ignored, never guessed at, and never
# contacted. See COMPLIANCE.md.
# Allowed values: groq
REPOS_AI_PRIMARY="groq"

# A second provider to try when the primary fails. Same allowed values; leave
# blank for no fallback.
REPOS_AI_FALLBACK=""

# Groq (https://console.groq.com) — free tier, plain API key, no OAuth.
GROQ_API_KEY=""
GROQ_MODEL="llama-3.3-70b-versatile"

# Hard timeout (ms) for any single AI call.
REPOS_AI_TIMEOUT_MS="45000"

# Set to "1" to hard-disable all outbound AI calls (fully offline mode).
REPOS_AI_DISABLED="0"

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Backups (OPTIONAL, largely vestigial on PostgreSQL)
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where RepOS looked for local database copies when it ran on SQLite. On
# PostgreSQL there is no local file to copy: backups and point-in-time
# recovery come from the Supabase project instead, and the in-app action says
# so rather than pretending. Still read by src/lib/backup/service.ts when
# listing whatever is already in that folder.
REPOS_BACKUP_DIR=""

# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test database (M20) — never set this in production
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The test suite ships no default connection string, on purpose: a working
# default would mean a database password in tracked source. Point this at a
# LOCAL, DISPOSABLE PostgreSQL database and nothing else — the suite creates
# and drops a schema per test file and truncates tables between cases. It
# refuses any host that is not localhost.
#
# Belongs in your shell, or in `.env.repos-test` — NOT in `.env.local`, which is
# where the real Supabase credentials live and which the test loader therefore
# refuses to read. `vitest.config.ts` reads `.env.repos-test` and copies only
# keys matching REPOS_TEST_*, so nothing else in that file could reach a test run
# even by accident. It is git-ignored by the same `.env*` rule as the rest.
REPOS_TEST_DATABASE_URL=""

# The SECOND connection string, and the one that matters most.
#
# Same cluster and same database as above, but connecting as the non-owner
# `repos_app` role — the one production uses, which cannot bypass RLS. It turns
# on tests/m20.runtime-role.test.ts, the only file that exercises RepOS with the
# policies actually in force. Without it the suite FAILS rather than skipping,
# on purpose: skipping is how signup, onboarding and password reset once shipped
# broken behind a green run.
REPOS_TEST_APP_DATABASE_URL=""
